A timer's `@tick` body is lowered like any other imperative body.

`timer` is an ordinary component, so the inliner has already put this one in the
window by the time passTimerPrimitive takes the node back out of the tree and
records the schedule on its owner. That owner is an `ir.Window`, and
`walkWindow` in internal/lower/walk.go -- the traversal passNoTernary,
passIndexedIter, passNoRef and NoDeclarative's id scan all ride on -- walked a
window's vars, funcs and body and not its timers. Nothing in source puts a timer
there, so the omission was invisible until the pass that creates one ran.

The ternary is the loud half: it reached the Go emitter intact and panicked the
build with "ir.Ternary reached Go codegen". The two-variable loop over a
`sngl:seq` range is the silent half -- a pull sequence hands out no ordinal, so
passIndexedIter counts one alongside, and without it Go got
`for i, x := range slices.Values(...)`, which is a compile error, and Kotlin got
an index bound to nothing.

The same body in an `@click` handler lowered correctly throughout, which is what
isolated the position rather than the constructs.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `for i, x := range` -- a pull sequence hands out no index; passIndexedIter counts one alongside
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `for i, x := range` -- the same, on the other Go platform whose timer is a primitive

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"
import seq "sngl:seq"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
}

component ticker ui.node {
    var level = 0.0
    var rung = 0

    time.timer(interval=50ms, @tick {
        level = level >= 1 ? 0.0 : level + 0.01
        for var i, x = seq.count(3) {
            rung = rung + i * x
        }
    })

    ui.text(value="{level} {rung}")
}

ui.window #main(title="tick") {
    ticker()
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
